#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf2e96a-8b3a-5175-a84c-79bb426aa3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305253} {:answer/id #uuid "1e830247-ccc1-59cf-8ab1-52e97736b3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305440} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2b201d-68ed-5c22-a614-3f40b0102596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306048} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4d336c-e643-5ced-921e-470ef6e9ebbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306068} {:answer/id #uuid "a41a73eb-f8d7-52b1-8075-5729f765c02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f4d336c-e643-5ced-921e-470ef6e9ebbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306068} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a891d9-a977-5225-95b8-2aaad9af9dd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0a1373b-0f2f-546e-8300-5cb2dc8f926e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334693" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+2{e}^{2x})^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1+4{e}^{2x}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+4{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{2x})^{′}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{3x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2{e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{3x})^{′}}+0 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f66a476-e65f-57ab-8b49-0ce935eee143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354928} {:answer/id #uuid "2562860b-9b12-5dc3-940b-b70009c64f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354976} {:answer/id #uuid "33dddaf2-ccc4-5a95-a6a4-01a05a938888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355122} {:answer/id #uuid "73340b9a-a4b7-52a6-beef-165ab017eb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355679} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b24e70-cfc1-54ae-aee2-0a7f0ebc97cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33dddaf2-ccc4-5a95-a6a4-01a05a938888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355122} :answer-field/id #uuid "c53bf1fa-e6e2-5962-8491-0b6cab04e1b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71ecf34a-b807-5183-a2fd-953c773f847a" :question/math-academy-id "q-89428" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{3}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(1,2)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(1,2)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,2),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(2{x}^{3}-y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =2(1)^{3}-(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =6(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3}+(2) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=0,\n$$\n\n we obtain that $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(1,2).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)}>0,\n$$\n\n we have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bddcdc3-0117-5789-a3ad-ba6aafc6b104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383377} {:answer/id #uuid "a1249cf7-75f0-5961-81c3-1869c734e389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383378} {:answer/id #uuid "efd31bc8-a127-522a-8023-054d96b8314f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383379} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f97e0c-20e3-563b-8b65-319653d528c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383380} {:answer/id #uuid "439ce31e-29f2-52ad-8058-a3a0cdd5a72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bddcdc3-0117-5789-a3ad-ba6aafc6b104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383377} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa5d990-d2c9-53e1-986d-feac4f9aa275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25122d3d-9cb1-5f5e-b327-d0566bb4de5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-136853" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.75\\cdot 0.8=0.6.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.05=0.25\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.15=0.75\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,1) & =0.20=0.25\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077fe147-bb8c-5245-adec-2f208cac3d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407342} {:answer/id #uuid "4e16abfb-19a2-5993-ba56-d6ea9e861af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407371} {:answer/id #uuid "8d91791d-f4c6-51bc-b004-2d1b8f7e6d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407404} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ada6aa-c40c-51ac-b29e-9b542bd9b596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407415} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d961f4-d0e1-5413-9854-bf9e8c1f9ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d91791d-f4c6-51bc-b004-2d1b8f7e6d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407404} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce80592a-d65e-5a24-ac6e-46f8654903b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f097a3f6-b1f4-531f-833b-43edfbbc8726" :question/math-academy-id "q-221882" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $6∣(42x+24y)$\n- $7∣(42x+24y)$\n- $2∣(42x+24y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=6(7x+4y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $7x+4y$ must be an integer, too. So, $6∣(42x+24y).$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $42(0)+24(1)=24,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=2(21x+12y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $21x+12y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2∣(42x+24y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fed5044-9dc6-5bdb-b912-3e1c49523233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409939} {:answer/id #uuid "233d2adf-428c-5279-b1bb-5fdd3b009f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409942} {:answer/id #uuid "803100ed-12d4-50e9-86ef-adf6635a27b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409961} {:answer/id #uuid "8839c5c1-33ac-599b-9b3b-008f227eed3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409965} {:answer/id #uuid "bed36c99-5a14-5541-a946-9449a10dcdbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233d2adf-428c-5279-b1bb-5fdd3b009f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409942} :answer-field/id #uuid "1528434c-93fa-59dc-b65b-46299ca0a431" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "615b6ed0-1120-5f2d-a6c0-2c676050f1ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-90846" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={3}^{x}?$\n\nI. The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$\n\nII. $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\nIII. $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/a2/a2a6352a60b420f8721d6c7e4553d3157cfb045a39b6a5119c11c5c8189023e2.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values greater than $y=0$, excluding $y=0$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Take, for example, $y=-1.$ Since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ),$ there can' t be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the function is not a surjection.  So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0083218c-aba9-5d4d-b323-2dc13bcc37a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354661} {:answer/id #uuid "bf668c7d-67c5-589e-9066-9cfa85ecdf23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356338} {:answer/id #uuid "c02ef90a-4b7d-5c3d-86af-85e9c052f575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356346} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e52708-e9c7-51c4-b416-ec80ac478adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356413} {:answer/id #uuid "f932dedf-4976-5e69-84af-ddcad303b44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0083218c-aba9-5d4d-b323-2dc13bcc37a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354661} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e85be0-6fb7-542e-a964-f2590b051fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb99fd8a-40c2-5ae9-b122-b1e03bf9e31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94465" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-7,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6315d0e1-42d4-5c4e-aba1-c811b0d9e0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355547} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9dc734-033b-5536-aeb2-bc5d613df824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355780} {:answer/id #uuid "d8498f0f-24f3-531e-bc2d-67ae855a8974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356560} {:answer/id #uuid "eacea395-10f3-5b0a-97f9-9e63077830b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356714} {:answer/id #uuid "f437379a-f3cf-5c0a-8f35-0368b6280e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f437379a-f3cf-5c0a-8f35-0368b6280e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356796} :answer-field/id #uuid "f04840df-52d1-5d7c-a82e-494bc20ca2e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a27d2cf-0561-5715-9600-290d8804741e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94466" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=9$ and $det(A)=18$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=9$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=9>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b1023c-e16f-5a0d-9093-f4068f33116d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357949} {:answer/id #uuid "19c030d7-744f-525b-9821-64d02a9665ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357978} {:answer/id #uuid "23e55a89-9b72-58a1-9bb3-18f3c2ba80d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358070} {:answer/id #uuid "5548d6d2-8ca2-5f9c-abc7-055657279605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358493} {:answer/id #uuid "e4264b76-4d84-5efd-8bdb-1605e0f2e213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19c030d7-744f-525b-9821-64d02a9665ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357978} :answer-field/id #uuid "9258361e-f4aa-5fce-b13d-158d1aa2d881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8be226ee-498f-567c-82d1-206ed205336b" :question/math-academy-id "q-96046" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a4ba53-acd7-5305-ab25-bfacca0b23df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309260} {:answer/id #uuid "773c9312-be0b-5424-ad66-40ee9439cabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309682} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab5dc75-7896-54ab-bb11-8f4d772b75bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309706} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e5764f-f500-540c-868d-2767df488781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310112} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9c7d96-0db0-52e5-8bef-fabdfc515ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ab5dc75-7896-54ab-bb11-8f4d772b75bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309706} :answer-field/id #uuid "82d27a9e-13d4-590a-b9ec-44b8440da9b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97cef292-5a85-5947-928d-d07517a20540" :question/math-academy-id "q-338489" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f$ is $\\pi $-periodic, shifting by $2\\pi $ does not change the value of $f,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f((x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\pi )=f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does not always equal $f(x).$ For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=F(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\ne f(x)$ for $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2723dc72-fd63-54a4-bb39-b1f78ca7b53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415206} {:answer/id #uuid "5c258c27-caa9-5d08-8078-126afe0392e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415217} {:answer/id #uuid "72010d15-094f-5946-8dbc-32f0cde47a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415223} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d0ed6f-03ab-58cb-b103-fad5d1a3eabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415245} {:answer/id #uuid "cc091e6f-2ffe-5090-bd21-1c547785f021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc091e6f-2ffe-5090-bd21-1c547785f021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415247} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd25f073-c3c7-52b2-85d6-ded0f2d4da84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77c64f72-bc4e-5149-99d1-c9f993fca3fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-125663" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x+y},\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $F$ is conservative.\n\nII. ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f2eb645-a4f1-5922-85bd-c5f103f512f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257350} {:answer/id #uuid "4d94cb3a-572c-5729-8df1-02ad9167fd86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257449} {:answer/id #uuid "70dc234c-6339-5727-b738-797b5cd95b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257774} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfb46fe-1c68-52b5-901b-f6d055f2ed25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258439} {:answer/id #uuid "f775cb4b-1fea-56f6-bcfd-066102a8734d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f2eb645-a4f1-5922-85bd-c5f103f512f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257350} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e83dc19-3afb-586c-8d39-b2cf5af58b97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "267c4ee8-beb7-545b-be5a-57b42fce6778" :question/math-academy-id "q-124111" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36996ffc-7dbc-51f8-8efe-46ed2c897045" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Affine Transformation is Orientation-Preserving"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c730df9-6548-5576-ba64-558226099add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364915} {:answer/id #uuid "b366d454-2c12-5c37-a81b-f48f317a5394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364916} {:answer/id #uuid "86c803ea-012c-568d-90f8-bce9b0ec9560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364917} {:answer/id #uuid "cb699b26-8700-53e1-89a0-b258c6ab715c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364918} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a37d2a-7bd5-528f-9255-1cf91b830b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c803ea-012c-568d-90f8-bce9b0ec9560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364917} :answer-field/id #uuid "3654e763-779e-5611-b4b5-b394a98f43a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91a59d9f-f2b5-5450-b850-f776f534f3f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124199" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=3u-v+2,\\,y=v-u.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $det(A)=-2$\n\nIII. The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v+2 \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e6bfcd-38c6-52e4-8b81-16a2ee609b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257073} {:answer/id #uuid "6aff1133-9d93-57b2-bdf9-2149c367e3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257725} {:answer/id #uuid "80d6cb62-0324-5c5d-99ae-4c9a206230fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257925} {:answer/id #uuid "8248f5bf-f517-5f8c-86de-f89163724871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257938} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e0b5ef-abd8-5ce4-84a4-4b4da2a4de2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2e0b5ef-abd8-5ce4-84a4-4b4da2a4de2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258493} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f3cfd9b-f898-53b9-910d-19385137f770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfac5beb-f3c0-5ece-80d7-1d1e280a780f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125432" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=-\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058c04c6-70ed-5f40-82ed-2d503073a803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416439} {:answer/id #uuid "21ea85fd-6292-5db7-9dc1-8fed164bc983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416440} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad14134-11d1-5b66-b73a-b208f1547b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416454} {:answer/id #uuid "dc81e283-4f32-52f2-9dc0-2d02dfad8da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416468} {:answer/id #uuid "ef49fb2b-88b6-550b-873b-58a76d4cd449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ad14134-11d1-5b66-b73a-b208f1547b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416454} :answer-field/id #uuid "2360b526-d791-5f7a-b1d6-add9140e9172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca5e4c86-b607-523c-a857-24250b844373" :question/math-academy-id "q-95299" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $6\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values\n- Any $10\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values.\n- The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ has two non-negative singular values." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $10\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 10$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 10}\\times {A}_{10\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, all singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5540b73e-76fe-5b3b-9e59-bcdd8e64db55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406815} {:answer/id #uuid "6da2df3c-89eb-5372-97ad-f660691bfcb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406817} {:answer/id #uuid "82cdbbed-d3b0-5cbd-98ec-7fd44334fdc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406819} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7f5f0b-b8d0-5234-8de6-49e97dadce22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406820} {:answer/id #uuid "d681f156-99f3-5753-a570-2bb3e53d88eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82cdbbed-d3b0-5cbd-98ec-7fd44334fdc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406819} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a41ad32-7f22-5720-8aeb-d6772bd219f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b3a8f74-a4d2-5020-9ea6-4077acac1b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47524" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,3x+2y-z=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$\n- If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible\n- If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Solving $3x+2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T},$ then \n\n$$\nT{v}_{1}=T{v}_{2}\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$ is a non-zero vector. This means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Null}(T)\\ne {0}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3db6b552-d8cb-5eb8-8ab9-86c9349c6a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272917} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0ab39c-6d94-5ca3-9930-01fc2c85e10c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273194} {:answer/id #uuid "670ec8c8-79d9-5ba1-a50b-e10f88c92b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273271} {:answer/id #uuid "85e4bdb1-8ef2-579b-a451-b627c7753440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273538} {:answer/id #uuid "fca063bf-2f9d-505a-bbdc-b4d448d753cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fca063bf-2f9d-505a-bbdc-b4d448d753cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274608} :answer-field/id #uuid "94c9e6d9-7389-51c0-ab13-de6c7eadca2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8f7202f-949a-5544-b1eb-75cc6a08bdaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-154696" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1$, we have $f(x)={e}^{1-x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{1-x}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-(-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
